1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
A simple substance that has literally become the building block of civilization.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:12,000
The main component of everything from roads and runways,

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:17,000
to buildings and bridges.

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Now, Concrete on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
110,000 tons of concrete were used to build the Seattle Kingdom in 1976.

6
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:58,520
Its enormous concrete roof spanned almost 8 acres and weighed 25,000 tons.

7
00:00:58,521 --> 00:01:05,420
Designed as a multi-purpose stadium, it acted as a concrete umbrella, protecting its occupants

8
00:01:05,421 --> 00:01:08,480
from the Pacific Northwest's notoriously fickle weather.

9
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:14,460
The Kingdome became an exclamation point for downtown Seattle.

10
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:20,000
And because of the concrete structure's durability, it was expected to stand the test of time.

11
00:01:21,140 --> 00:01:28,500
Eight, seven, six, five, four, three, two, one.

12
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:40,460
But it was not to be.

13
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:46,000
It took just seconds for dynamite to demolish what had taken years to design and build.

14
00:01:47,060 --> 00:01:49,460
On a Sunday morning in March 2000,

15
00:01:49,461 --> 00:01:54,660
the Kingdome's concrete was blown to bits in a rapid-fire series of detonations.

16
00:01:54,661 --> 00:02:00,700
53,000 cubic yards of concrete imploded in the blink of an eye.

17
00:02:02,980 --> 00:02:09,240
When you watch the Kingdome in Seattle as it's imploding, being demolished with a big explosion,

18
00:02:10,040 --> 00:02:14,980
you have to say, why? Why has it only been 30 years that we've used this building?

19
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:20,720
Well, it happens that it outgrew, the needs outgrew the structure.

20
00:02:20,721 --> 00:02:29,360
Over the years, the Kingdome's concrete had undergone some extensive renovations, but its strength and durability were never questioned.

21
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:36,740
When Seattle's football team was sold in the late 1990s, its new owner was guaranteed a new stadium.

22
00:02:37,340 --> 00:02:39,060
That sealed the Kingdome's fate.

23
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:43,620
It doesn't mean that the concrete materials have gone bad.

24
00:02:43,621 --> 00:02:48,940
It's no different than many of the other buildings that have been taken down in order to make

25
00:02:48,941 --> 00:02:52,800
way for new structures as we have new needs in the community.

26
00:02:52,801 --> 00:03:02,020
This has been happening throughout the ages.

27
00:03:02,021 --> 00:03:08,860
And throughout the ages, it is the development and use of concrete that has made it all possible.

28
00:03:08,861 --> 00:03:12,322
Well, concrete is a composite material.

29
00:03:12,323 --> 00:03:18,980
It's an artificial stone, and it is made of sand, stone, water, and cement.

30
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:27,180
Each of its ingredients is essential, but it is the chemical composition of cement that makes concrete possible.

31
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:34,260
There's a saying in the concrete industry that cement is to concrete as flour is to cake.

32
00:03:34,261 --> 00:03:41,260
If concrete begins with cement, then cement begins at the rock quarry.

33
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:48,260
Huge rocks and boulders are crushed into a rough gravel.

34
00:03:48,261 --> 00:03:53,260
At a cement plant they're ground into a fine powder which is mostly limestone.

35
00:03:53,261 --> 00:03:57,160
It's then mixed with small amounts of calcium, silicon,

36
00:03:57,161 --> 00:04:04,160
aluminum and iron. This material known as raw meal, is now ready for the kiln.

37
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:06,140
a huge cylindrical furnace.

38
00:04:07,860 --> 00:04:12,460
A modern kiln is 100 yards long, the length of a football field.

39
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:16,780
It's the world's largest moving piece of equipment and one of the hottest.

40
00:04:17,560 --> 00:04:19,920
The raw meal enters the kiln's upper end.

41
00:04:21,260 --> 00:04:24,840
As it turns, the material moves through progressively hotter zones.

42
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:27,940
As it tumbles down the rotating kiln,

43
00:04:28,340 --> 00:04:31,860
it is heated to a temperature of 2,700 degrees Fahrenheit

44
00:04:31,861 --> 00:04:33,800
and becomes partially molten.

45
00:04:34,660 --> 00:04:39,100
Under this intense heat, individual molecules break apart and recombine

46
00:04:39,101 --> 00:04:42,400
to form a new marble-sized component called clinker.

47
00:04:43,480 --> 00:04:46,920
Once it is cooled, the clinker is mixed with a small amount of gypsum,

48
00:04:47,380 --> 00:04:50,920
then ground into the superfine powder known as Portland cement.

49
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:55,440
It got its name because it resembled stone from the island of Portland, England.

50
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:01,040
Portland cement is the thing that binds everything together.

51
00:05:01,860 --> 00:05:09,040
To create concrete, the cement is blended together with water, forming a paste that coats the surface of the final ingredients.

52
00:05:10,380 --> 00:05:13,380
Sand and hard core stones known as aggregate.

53
00:05:15,100 --> 00:05:22,100
When water and cement mix, it begins a process called hydration, which is a chemical process, changing its properties.

54
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:26,780
It goes from a very plastic liquid state to a hardened state.

55
00:05:26,781 --> 00:05:34,340
And that process is what combines all of the sand and the gravel and the cement together into this hard mass.

56
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:39,640
Concrete has evolved into a critical component of modern civilization.

57
00:05:40,920 --> 00:05:42,422
You see concrete every day.

58
00:05:42,423 --> 00:05:48,040
You walk on concrete sidewalks, you drive down concrete roads, you go to work in a concrete building.

59
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:50,200
Some people go home to live in a concrete house.

60
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:54,300
Concrete is the building block of civilization.

61
00:05:54,301 --> 00:05:58,460
Concrete is as ancient as it is modern.

62
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:06,240
The history of cementing materials together goes back to the time when prehistoric man abandoned his caves to build shelters.

63
00:06:07,460 --> 00:06:13,540
He used mud and clay to fill in the gaps between stones to keep out the wind and the cold.

64
00:06:14,840 --> 00:06:18,440
Later, the Assyrians and Babylonians used clay mixed with straw.

65
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:25,120
The straw gave the earthen material packed around it a rough skeletal framework and allowed it to be shaped.

66
00:06:25,980 --> 00:06:33,580
The Egyptians used lime and gypsum, a mineral rich in calcium sulfate, to create a material that would harden even better.

67
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:45,180
The Egyptians may have been using lime and crushed stone to cast certain shapes, small shapes, and use them on their buildings and monuments.

68
00:06:45,181 --> 00:06:50,780
but they had not discovered concrete as we know it today, which is a much stronger material.

69
00:06:52,460 --> 00:06:54,500
The Greeks made further improvements.

70
00:06:55,420 --> 00:07:00,420
Then the Romans perfected a cement that produced structures of remarkable durability.

71
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:07,640
We have to go back to the Roman Empire and the ingenuity of the Romans.

72
00:07:08,120 --> 00:07:12,420
And the aqueducts, to me, are probably the best and the greatest thing they did.

73
00:07:12,421 --> 00:07:18,260
It was only in the ash of the volcanoes that developed the Roman concrete.

74
00:07:20,040 --> 00:07:23,420
That volcanic ash would come to be known as poslin.

75
00:07:24,740 --> 00:07:31,640
It is a very fine dust, which is good for the concrete because you've got a lot of surface area to it.

76
00:07:32,140 --> 00:07:36,480
And so when they took the poslin and mixed it up like they would put it on the wall,

77
00:07:36,481 --> 00:07:43,900
holy moly, all of a sudden they get a real material that will set hard underwater.

78
00:07:46,100 --> 00:07:50,460
Pozzolin was the key ingredient in what would become known as Roman concrete.

79
00:07:51,160 --> 00:07:53,740
It was essential in building the Pantheon.

80
00:07:54,700 --> 00:07:59,120
The Pantheon is a building that is a round building.

81
00:07:59,400 --> 00:08:05,020
It's 100 feet high and has a dome of 143 feet in diameter.

82
00:08:05,021 --> 00:08:08,560
Now that's a big building for any engineer to look at.

83
00:08:09,100 --> 00:08:10,320
How did they accomplish this?

84
00:08:10,360 --> 00:08:15,580
This was a huge open space with a single domed closing on the top.

85
00:08:16,140 --> 00:08:18,420
And yet, that was 2,000 years ago.

86
00:08:19,020 --> 00:08:27,120
Well, the Roman engineering really developed quite a bit in the highway, in the water, transportation, and in the materials area.

87
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:30,660
They learned very specifically how to handle concrete.

88
00:08:30,661 --> 00:08:36,340
While the best examples of Roman architecture still exist today,

89
00:08:36,900 --> 00:08:39,700
whatever knowledge their architects had disappeared

90
00:08:39,701 --> 00:08:42,980
when their empire faded into the pages of history.

91
00:08:43,940 --> 00:08:47,320
The quality control features of making concrete was forgotten.

92
00:08:47,680 --> 00:08:49,400
It's as simple as it was.

93
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:53,740
They lost the recipe on to control the quality of their product.

94
00:08:54,960 --> 00:09:00,020
During the Middle Ages, European architects concentrated mainly on building castles

95
00:09:00,021 --> 00:09:03,560
and cathedrals of huge stone instead of concrete.

96
00:09:04,820 --> 00:09:09,040
It wasn't until almost 1800 years after the fall of the Roman Empire

97
00:09:09,041 --> 00:09:14,360
that an experiment by English chemist John Smeaton led to a true discovery.

98
00:09:15,180 --> 00:09:21,640
In 1756, Smeaton was given the job of rebuilding the perpetually crumbling Eddystone lighthouse.

99
00:09:21,641 --> 00:09:35,640
He used some of the real clay limestone sources that he cooked or burnt to make a material.

100
00:09:35,641 --> 00:09:38,640
It was called hydraulic lime.

101
00:09:38,641 --> 00:09:44,640
And he used this for his blocks mortar he needed in the lighthouse.

102
00:09:44,641 --> 00:09:49,640
It wasn't a very outstanding material, but it worked underwater.

103
00:09:49,641 --> 00:09:53,286
water. I think it was a definitive moment.

104
00:09:53,287 --> 00:09:57,540
Mortar is the plaster that holds stones together.

105
00:09:58,280 --> 00:10:05,000
In order to create a material that binds properly, many scientists experimented with grinding

106
00:10:05,001 --> 00:10:07,563
limestone and clay together.

107
00:10:07,564 --> 00:10:13,420
But it wasn't until 1824 when another Englishman, Joseph Aspton,

108
00:10:13,421 --> 00:10:15,077
made a major discovery.

109
00:10:15,078 --> 00:10:20,480
He burned his mixture of limestone and clay into lumps and then crushed the

110
00:10:20,481 --> 00:10:22,196
lumps into a fine powder.

111
00:10:22,197 --> 00:10:27,140
When he mixed it with water and sand, it made a strong mortar that would

112
00:10:27,141 --> 00:10:32,678
harden underwater. Aspton obtained a patent for his product in 1824.

113
00:10:32,679 --> 00:10:35,040
He called it Portland Cement.

114
00:10:36,040 --> 00:10:40,660
His claim to fame is that he patented the process that these other people had been doing

115
00:10:40,661 --> 00:10:42,660
for the last 50 years.

116
00:10:44,300 --> 00:10:47,640
Portland cement would come to define the concrete industry.

117
00:10:48,400 --> 00:10:51,640
But the next 50 years would lead to a series of new developments

118
00:10:51,641 --> 00:10:55,800
that would change the use of concrete and the world forever.

119
00:10:59,540 --> 00:11:02,820
The top three cement-producing countries in the world

120
00:11:02,821 --> 00:11:06,440
are China, Japan, and the United States.

121
00:11:06,441 --> 00:11:10,880
Concrete will return on Modern Marvels

122
00:11:10,881 --> 00:11:16,440
We now return to Concrete on Modern Marvels

123
00:11:17,360 --> 00:11:22,140
At the beginning of the 19th century

124
00:11:22,141 --> 00:11:26,520
the United States was still in its relative infancy as an independent nation

125
00:11:26,521 --> 00:11:29,200
But with its ties to England broken

126
00:11:29,201 --> 00:11:32,220
America had begun a period of rapid expansion

127
00:11:32,221 --> 00:11:35,260
that had to rely on its own natural resources

128
00:11:35,261 --> 00:11:39,040
coal, minerals, timber, and water.

129
00:11:42,220 --> 00:11:46,820
The Erie Canal seemed to me to be a real starting point.

130
00:11:47,640 --> 00:11:51,860
Construction of the Erie Canal began in the state of New York in 1817.

131
00:11:52,800 --> 00:11:59,160
It was designed to be a vast transportation system stretching 360 miles long.

132
00:11:59,980 --> 00:12:03,920
The waterway would have more than 80 changes in elevation called lifts.

133
00:12:05,260 --> 00:12:12,580
Well, these lifts required structures, and the structures required hydraulic, lime to put them together.

134
00:12:13,320 --> 00:12:20,100
So there was a demand, and it just so happens that they found a natural cement that could be heated.

135
00:12:21,900 --> 00:12:27,220
Erie Canal engineer Canvas White experimented with different kinds of rocks in the area,

136
00:12:27,940 --> 00:12:32,200
heating and then crushing them into a coarse powder, which he then mixed with water.

137
00:12:32,201 --> 00:12:38,680
Builders would soon begin batching their own versions of White's crude formula on a variety of structures.

138
00:12:39,200 --> 00:12:47,960
They used stones of any kind, sometimes even crushed oyster shells, to create a paste that could hold a wall together when it hardened.

139
00:12:47,961 --> 00:13:01,961
In the past 200 years, there has been a very rapid growth starting in the 1840s with steel, with glass, with aluminum, with ceramics, with rubbers and concrete.

140
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:10,000
I think, in fact, that our world civilization reached a point that we needed these materials.

141
00:13:10,001 --> 00:13:21,720
By the 1850s, Portland cement had become widely used in Europe, and it was there that a major breakthrough was made that would forever change concrete construction.

142
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:28,040
The first use of iron to strengthen concrete by reinforcing it internally.

143
00:13:28,041 --> 00:13:39,499
Reinforced concrete is concrete in combination with some other reinforcing material, usually steel, that when used with concrete actually works to resist both

144
00:13:39,500 --> 00:13:42,400
compression and tension in the concrete.

145
00:13:43,440 --> 00:13:46,700
Tension is a pulling force that acts in one direction.

146
00:13:47,520 --> 00:13:50,320
An example is the force of a cable that is holding a weight.

147
00:13:50,321 --> 00:13:58,400
Under tension, a material usually stretches and returns to its original form if the force doesn't exceed the material's limit.

148
00:13:59,940 --> 00:14:03,740
Greater forces create greater tension, which can rupture the material.

149
00:14:04,560 --> 00:14:09,380
Compression is the decrease in a material's volume that results from the application of pressure.

150
00:14:10,160 --> 00:14:13,260
That pressure can come from one or several sources.

151
00:14:14,620 --> 00:14:18,040
Tensile strength is the ability for a material to resist pulling or tension.

152
00:14:18,041 --> 00:14:23,280
Pulling is simply pulling material so that you're trying to pull it apart,

153
00:14:23,281 --> 00:14:26,040
as opposed to compression, where you're trying to push it together.

154
00:14:26,041 --> 00:14:31,840
Concrete, historically, and just by nature, is stronger in compression than it is in tension.

155
00:14:33,340 --> 00:14:37,840
The invention of reinforced concrete is usually attributed to a Frenchman,

156
00:14:38,460 --> 00:14:43,760
Joseph Monnier, who made garden pots and tubs with concrete reinforced by iron mesh.

157
00:14:44,640 --> 00:14:47,440
Monnier patented his discovery in 1867.

158
00:14:48,040 --> 00:14:55,040
Pretty soon, others in the building trade saw it and began to think of how they could use the material elsewhere.

159
00:14:55,041 --> 00:14:59,040
If you could make a pot that strong and that thin and that light,

160
00:14:59,041 --> 00:15:04,040
imagine what we could do in the walls of a house or the roof of a cathedral.

161
00:15:04,041 --> 00:15:10,040
The first reinforced concrete structure in the United States was a mansion in New York

162
00:15:10,041 --> 00:15:15,040
that made extensive use of concrete beams reinforced by iron joists.

163
00:15:15,041 --> 00:15:21,220
When designers begin to understand how to design reinforced concrete members,

164
00:15:22,160 --> 00:15:26,880
the use of concrete for structures actually expanded significantly.

165
00:15:27,580 --> 00:15:32,780
Industrial expansion called for a stronger and more uniform material than natural cement.

166
00:15:33,560 --> 00:15:40,200
By the 1870s, American engineers have begun to specify the use of European Portland cement for their projects.

167
00:15:40,201 --> 00:15:47,200
A Pennsylvania man was convinced he could produce Portland cement in the United States

168
00:15:47,201 --> 00:15:51,200
that was just as good as the German and English cement being imported.

169
00:15:51,201 --> 00:16:00,200
In 1874, David Saylor founded the Copley Cement Company in the Lehigh Valley area of Pennsylvania.

170
00:16:00,201 --> 00:16:06,200
It would come to be known as the birthplace of the U.S. cement industry.

171
00:16:06,201 --> 00:16:10,760
60-foot-tall brick kilns were built to handle the increasing demand.

172
00:16:12,100 --> 00:16:16,260
Soon, stationary kilns would be replaced by rotary ones.

173
00:16:17,900 --> 00:16:22,640
This way, you could continually, from the mine, you could keep dumping rocks into this kiln forever.

174
00:16:23,260 --> 00:16:29,760
And it would continuously create, you didn't have to cook the thing for 24 hours, empty it out, and then restart a new batch.

175
00:16:30,180 --> 00:16:32,960
You're essentially batching the stuff continuously.

176
00:16:32,961 --> 00:16:39,960
It was around this time that Thomas Edison took an active interest in concrete.

177
00:16:39,961 --> 00:16:46,960
In 1902, the famous American inventor founded the Edison Portland Cement Works in New Jersey.

178
00:16:46,961 --> 00:16:50,960
Edison also had this great idea that he could preform a house.

179
00:16:50,961 --> 00:16:53,960
He could make a prefabricated house out of concrete.

180
00:16:53,961 --> 00:16:58,960
He thought he could make a set of very complicated forms that were expensive at the time,

181
00:16:58,961 --> 00:17:04,660
and that he could cast the house and then just take all the formwork off go to the next place and make another house

182
00:17:06,800 --> 00:17:11,220
Edison completed several homes and boasted that they would last for centuries

183
00:17:11,940 --> 00:17:15,260
The process didn't catch on though, but once again

184
00:17:16,280 --> 00:17:18,280
Edison had the right idea

185
00:17:19,460 --> 00:17:23,720
History has proven Thomas Edison right concrete is a very durable material

186
00:17:23,721 --> 00:17:30,220
It's a very economical material, a very strong material, and we still can go back and look at Thomas Edison's houses today.

187
00:17:31,500 --> 00:17:36,740
Within a few years, reinforced concrete had become an economical substitute for stone,

188
00:17:37,180 --> 00:17:40,480
since it was cheaper to produce than stone was to quarry.

189
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:48,180
Designers and builders began work on structures that utilized concrete's new architectural companion, steel.

190
00:17:49,180 --> 00:17:53,060
When the Ingalls building went up in Cincinnati, Ohio in 1902,

191
00:17:53,560 --> 00:17:55,820
it became the world's first skyscraper.

192
00:17:56,720 --> 00:17:59,860
It was a 16-story, 210-foot building,

193
00:18:00,200 --> 00:18:03,460
and it was twice as tall as any concrete building had been before it.

194
00:18:04,020 --> 00:18:05,760
There's a story about a newspaper reporter

195
00:18:05,761 --> 00:18:08,160
who waited across the street from the building all night

196
00:18:08,161 --> 00:18:11,060
because he was convinced that it was going to collapse of its own weight.

197
00:18:11,520 --> 00:18:13,940
So people really were in complete disbelief.

198
00:18:14,300 --> 00:18:17,380
They really didn't believe that this building would stand, and it did.

199
00:18:17,480 --> 00:18:18,180
It shocked everyone.

200
00:18:18,181 --> 00:18:25,500
When the Ingalls building was first designed and built, we had very limited understanding

201
00:18:25,501 --> 00:18:30,460
engineering-wise as to the behavior of the material and the behavior of the composite

202
00:18:30,461 --> 00:18:34,960
material of the reinforcing steel working in conjunction with the concrete.

203
00:18:36,420 --> 00:18:41,980
In engineering, the term tensile strength refers to an object's resistance to forces

204
00:18:41,981 --> 00:18:43,580
that threaten to break it apart.

205
00:18:43,581 --> 00:18:50,580
Concrete, though strong in substance, is weak in tensile strength.

206
00:18:50,581 --> 00:18:59,580
So if I had a beam and I put a load on the top of the beam, if it did not have reinforcing,

207
00:18:59,581 --> 00:19:04,580
the beam would crack very easily and, of course, collapse.

208
00:19:04,581 --> 00:19:12,580
However, if we put a steel bar inside the concrete and we take this beam and we put load on top of it,

209
00:19:12,581 --> 00:19:21,580
on top of it, the reinforcing bar becomes in tension and can support a much greater load

210
00:19:21,581 --> 00:19:23,580
than an unreinforced concrete beam.

211
00:19:23,581 --> 00:19:31,580
While America's skylines were changing, so were its roads.

212
00:19:31,581 --> 00:19:37,580
Up until now, roads were made of dirt, gravel, stone or brick.

213
00:19:37,581 --> 00:19:46,180
The first concrete road in the United States was built in Bella Fontaine, Ohio, by George Bartholomew in 1894.

214
00:19:47,720 --> 00:19:52,260
The American public definitely needed to be sold on the use of concrete as a paving material.

215
00:19:53,760 --> 00:19:58,780
At first, only a small section of Bella Fontaine's Main Street was paved with concrete.

216
00:19:59,920 --> 00:20:03,440
City officials were skeptical. They thought it would crumble and crack.

217
00:20:03,441 --> 00:20:08,160
They required a guarantee that the pavement would last at least five years.

218
00:20:08,700 --> 00:20:10,340
It did, and then some.

219
00:20:14,280 --> 00:20:17,920
While horses and wagons soon gave way to motorized vehicles,

220
00:20:18,480 --> 00:20:21,160
the American cement industry was changing too.

221
00:20:21,860 --> 00:20:25,440
What began as an informal business association in Chicago, Illinois,

222
00:20:25,960 --> 00:20:29,020
